    "abstract": "When children work, their education competes for their time and effort. This paper develops and estimates a structural model in which Mexican students finishing primary school choose whether to enroll in secondary school, whether to work while in school, and how much study effort to exert. Choices depend on local school options, travel distance, and local labor market wages. The model is estimated using test scores, survey data from students and parents, school locations, and census-derived wage data. These estimates are then used to investigate the key barriers to students’ academic success and evaluate a range of educational policies. Counterfactual exercises show that even modest enforcement of child labor restrictions raises enrollment by more than eliminating travel distance entirely, showing the importance of opportunity costs. Results also show significant heterogeneity in student effort choices, with effort being more productive in high-quality schools. This heterogeneity implies that policies equalizing school access cannot fully equalize students’ educational outcomes without also addressing differences in school quality. The paper also evaluates alternative conditional cash transfer program designs, some of which condition transfers on academic performance.",
