    "abstract": "Europe’s fragmented business landscape can make it difficult for firms to expand across borders and reach scale. This column examines the economic case for a 28th European enterprise regime: an optional EU-wide legal framework covering firm formation, financing, and insolvency. Model-based simulations suggest that a comprehensive regime could raise EU GDP by around 3.3% after ten years and 4.4% after twenty years, with the ten-year gain equivalent to almost 15% of the current EU-US hourly labour productivity gap. The results also highlight the importance of covering the full firm life cycle, including restructuring and insolvency.",
