    "abstract": "Welfare benefit sanctions are a common tool in many social insurance systems. Whether political pressure shapes enforcement within formally rule-based welfare programs, particularly in strong-institution settings, remains poorly understood. We study how the political composition of a parliamentary constituency affects unemployment benefit sanction rates in the United Kingdom, exploiting close elections in a regression discontinuity design. Following a major reform that tightened the JSA sanction regime in 2012, constituencies represented by government-aligned MPs experienced sanction rates 18% lower than comparable Labour-held seats—a drop of around 0.8 percentage points at the electoral threshold, driven by fewer sanctions imposed rather than fewer claimants. Effects emerge only after the 2012 reform and are concentrated in newly flipped seats, where electoral stakes are highest. This pattern is consistent with electoral incentives and partisan alignment shaping the local implementation of a national, rule-based welfare policy.",
